// LeetCode 276 bench kernel — BRUTE-FORCE ENUMERATION, not the DP.
//
// WHY THE DP IS NOT THE WORKLOAD. The two-state solver is O(n) and `n` cannot
// exceed 89 before the answer leaves i64 — about ninety iterations of two adds
// and two multiplies. There is nothing there to time; any measurement would be
// loop overhead and process startup. Benching it would have meant inventing a
// modular variant, which is a different problem wearing this kata's name.
//
// The kata already contains a real workload. `paint_fence_brute.kara` walks all
// `k^n` paintings and rejects three-in-a-row: O(n·k^n), branch-heavy, array-
// indexed, and entirely data-dependent. At n=13, k=4 that is 67 million
// paintings and ~800 million comparisons. That is worth measuring, and it is the
// program the kata already ships rather than something built to be benched.
//
// THE SINK IS THE ANSWER, which is the useful property here. Enumeration and the
// DP compute the same number by unrelated means, so the bench verifies itself:
// the printed total must equal `num_ways(13, 4)`, and a lane that vectorized
// away half the search would say so immediately. No separate oracle needed.
//
// PARALLEL LANE. The enumeration splits on the first two posts: fix `c[0]` and
// `c[1]`, odometer only positions 2..n-1, and the k² = 16 prefixes are wholly
// independent — each branch owns its own array and reads nothing shared. The
// three-in-a-row rule does NOT couple them, because each branch checks its whole
// n-length array including its fixed prefix; a painting is examined by exactly
// the one branch that owns its first two posts.
//
// `paint_enum_seq.kara` is this file without the attribute. That seq/par pair is
// the only Kāra-to-Kāra comparison drawn — a row against a mirror that never
// opted into parallelism conflates compiler quality with the comparator's
// choice.
//
// SIXTEEN BRANCHES against 18 cores is a deliberate under-fill. The split is
// k² and k is fixed by the workload, so the alternatives are k=4 → 16 or a
// three-post split at k³ = 64 (four waves of 16, same imbalance, more overhead).
// 16 leaves two cores idle and is still the best available factoring.

fn count_prefix(n: i64, k: i64, p0: i64, p1: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut c: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    let mut z = 0;
    while z < n {
        c.push(0);
        z = z + 1;
    }
    c[0] = p0;
    c[1] = p1;
    let mut count = 0;
    let mut more = true;
    while more {
        let mut ok = true;
        let mut i = 2;
        while i < n {
            if c[i] == c[i - 1] and c[i - 1] == c[i - 2] {
                ok = false;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if ok { count = count + 1; }

        // odometer over the SUFFIX only — positions 0 and 1 are this branch's
        let mut p = n - 1;
        while p >= 2 and c[p] == k - 1 {
            c[p] = 0;
            p = p - 1;
        }
        if p < 2 { more = false; } else { c[p] = c[p] + 1; }
    }
    return count;
}

fn main() {
    let n = 13;
    let k = 4;

    let mut parts: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    #[par_order_free]
    for pre in 0..(k * k) {
        if pre >= 0 { parts.push(count_prefix(n, k, pre / k, pre % k)); }
    }

    let mut total = 0;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < parts.len() {
        total = total + parts[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    println(total);
}
